  "timing_notes": "Each trial repeats the same complete case batch, resetting inputs for one native call; no trajectory is advanced between repetitions. Both implementations evaluate two levels per case, above and within the boundary layer. Fortran includes copying fixed inputs, setting species/flags, native grid setup, both correction passes and final output copies. JAX computes the grid and all native outputs in the compiled call, dispatches each static configuration group, then synchronizes every physical output once per complete batch. Immutable JAX inputs require no reset copies. The timed JAX wrapper returns exactly the seven native caller fields; internal volume/core/diameter/valid diagnostics are checked outside timing and unused outputs may be eliminated. Compilation, initial transfers, native process startup/I/O, checksum and comparison work are excluded from warm timing. Fortran uses separately compiled O2 source without fast-math/LTO, runtime checks disabled; checked and optimized outputs must agree exactly before any trials. Trial order alternates. Thread settings are recorded, not standardized. This is composed microphysics performance, not complete HAM box-model performance."
}
